1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:05,020
Throw a coin into the Trevi Fountain, and
it lands in water that started flowing to

2
00:00:05,060 --> 00:00:06,610
Rome in nineteen BC.

3
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:07,820
Same springs.

4
00:00:07,860 --> 00:00:08,810
Still running.

5
00:00:09,577 --> 00:00:14,047
Ancient Rome pulled rivers of fresh water
into a city of around a million people.

6
00:00:14,337 --> 00:00:14,947
No pumps.

7
00:00:15,057 --> 00:00:17,607
Just a slope so gentle
you can't even see it.

8
00:00:18,371 --> 00:00:20,941
Then, one year, an army
switched it all off.

9
00:00:21,131 --> 00:00:24,421
And it took Rome almost a thousand
years to get its water back.

10
00:00:24,551 --> 00:00:25,901
So how did the water get in?

11
00:00:25,951 --> 00:00:26,901
And how did it stop?

12
00:00:29,232 --> 00:00:30,922
Rome sits on the river Tiber.

13
00:00:31,032 --> 00:00:33,422
But for drinking, the
Tiber was a bad idea.

14
00:00:33,712 --> 00:00:35,452
The city's sewers emptied into it.

15
00:00:35,492 --> 00:00:36,482
And it often flooded.

16
00:00:37,308 --> 00:00:39,788
So Rome went looking for
clean springs in the hills,

17
00:00:39,828 --> 00:00:41,818
some more than fifty miles away.

18
00:00:42,208 --> 00:00:45,548
Starting in three hundred and twelve
BC, over the next five centuries,

19
00:00:45,588 --> 00:00:48,438
it built eleven aqueducts
to bring that water in.

20
00:00:51,292 --> 00:00:52,302
Here's the secret.

21
00:00:52,412 --> 00:00:55,712
An aqueduct is just a channel
that runs very slightly downhill,

22
00:00:55,752 --> 00:00:57,862
all the way from the spring to the city.

23
00:00:58,152 --> 00:00:58,822
No pumps.

24
00:00:59,092 --> 00:01:00,142
Gravity does everything.

25
00:01:01,087 --> 00:01:02,587
The hard part is the slope.

26
00:01:02,627 --> 00:01:05,677
Too steep, and the water rushes
and wears the channel away.

27
00:01:05,847 --> 00:01:07,697
Too flat, and it stops flowing.

28
00:01:08,067 --> 00:01:12,307
So Roman surveyors had to plan a gentle,
steady drop over dozens of miles of hills

29
00:01:12,347 --> 00:01:13,017
and valleys.

30
00:01:13,995 --> 00:01:16,455
Look at the aqueduct that
supplied the city of Nimes,

31
00:01:16,495 --> 00:01:17,825
in southern France.

32
00:01:18,055 --> 00:01:21,785
Over about thirty miles, it drops
only around forty feet in total.

33
00:01:22,627 --> 00:01:25,007
Its most famous bridge, the Pont du Gard,

34
00:01:25,047 --> 00:01:27,197
is about a hundred and sixty feet high.

35
00:01:27,467 --> 00:01:30,057
Along its whole length,
roughly fifteen hundred feet,

36
00:01:30,167 --> 00:01:32,877
the water channel on top
drops by just one inch.

37
00:01:33,810 --> 00:01:35,940
The lower arches were
built without mortar.

38
00:01:36,210 --> 00:01:38,550
Huge stone blocks, some
weighing several tons,

39
00:01:38,590 --> 00:01:41,660
held in place by their own
weight and their perfect cut.

40
00:01:42,363 --> 00:01:46,773
Without lasers or GPS, Roman
surveyors did this with simple tools:

41
00:01:46,963 --> 00:01:49,863
a long wooden table with a water
level, called a chorobates,

42
00:01:49,903 --> 00:01:51,993
and a sighting cross called a groma.

43
00:01:52,968 --> 00:01:54,898
Here's something most people get wrong.

44
00:01:55,148 --> 00:01:57,018
The famous arches are the exception.

45
00:01:57,388 --> 00:02:00,928
Most of an aqueduct runs underground,
in tunnels and buried channels,

46
00:02:00,968 --> 00:02:02,618
following the shape of the hills.

47
00:02:03,208 --> 00:02:05,758
Arches were built only where
the water had to cross a valley.

48
00:02:06,647 --> 00:02:09,117
Underground, the water
stayed cool and clean,

49
00:02:09,227 --> 00:02:10,457
and it was safe from enemies.

50
00:02:11,007 --> 00:02:15,097
Workers could climb down through shafts
every few hundred feet to clean the channel.

51
00:02:15,307 --> 00:02:19,487
But one day, an enemy army would try to
use these channels as a secret way into

52
00:02:19,527 --> 00:02:19,957
Rome.

53
00:02:20,891 --> 00:02:23,231
Where a valley was too
deep even for arches,

54
00:02:23,271 --> 00:02:27,691
the Romans used a trick: sealed pipes that
let the water run down one side of the

55
00:02:27,731 --> 00:02:30,581
valley and back up the other,
pushed by its own pressure.

56
00:02:31,271 --> 00:02:33,321
Engineers call it an inverted siphon.

57
00:02:36,098 --> 00:02:38,388
When the water reached
Rome, who got it first?

58
00:02:38,638 --> 00:02:42,518
It flowed into a distribution tank, and
from there into the city in a strict order

59
00:02:42,558 --> 00:02:43,368
of priority.

60
00:02:44,430 --> 00:02:47,880
First, public fountains, where
anyone could fill a jar for free.

61
00:02:48,130 --> 00:02:49,520
Then, the public baths.

62
00:02:49,610 --> 00:02:53,180
And only last, private homes, for
the rich who paid for their own pipe.

63
00:02:54,061 --> 00:02:55,751
The fountains never had taps.

64
00:02:56,001 --> 00:02:58,131
The water ran all the time, day and night,

65
00:02:58,181 --> 00:02:59,731
and the overflow was useful too.

66
00:02:59,941 --> 00:03:01,671
It flushed the streets and the sewers.

67
00:03:02,356 --> 00:03:05,666
Rome had huge public baths
with hot and cold pools,

68
00:03:05,736 --> 00:03:08,776
and public toilets with
dozens of seats side by side,

69
00:03:08,816 --> 00:03:10,306
where people sat and chatted.

70
00:03:11,109 --> 00:03:12,519
The baths were enormous.

71
00:03:12,889 --> 00:03:15,829
The Baths of Caracalla, which
still stand in ruins in Rome,

72
00:03:15,869 --> 00:03:18,349
could take more than a
thousand bathers at a time,

73
00:03:18,389 --> 00:03:20,539
with libraries, gardens and gyms.

74
00:03:20,689 --> 00:03:22,599
All of it filled by aqueduct water.

75
00:03:25,547 --> 00:03:26,917
Where there's free
water, there are thieves.

76
00:03:27,482 --> 00:03:32,062
In the year ninety-seven AD, a senator
named Frontinus became Rome's water

77
00:03:32,102 --> 00:03:32,732
commissioner.

78
00:03:33,002 --> 00:03:35,342
And he wrote a whole book
about how the system worked,

79
00:03:35,382 --> 00:03:37,032
and about what was going wrong.

80
00:03:37,920 --> 00:03:41,530
He found that a lot of water never
reached the people it was meant for.

81
00:03:41,840 --> 00:03:44,410
Landowners tapped into the
aqueducts in the countryside.

82
00:03:44,900 --> 00:03:48,610
In the city, water workers were
secretly paid to connect private pipes.

83
00:03:49,492 --> 00:03:51,022
Those pipes were made of lead.

84
00:03:51,152 --> 00:03:53,142
The Latin word for lead is plumbum.

85
00:03:53,232 --> 00:03:55,222
And that's where our
word plumbing comes from.

86
00:03:55,819 --> 00:03:59,889
Pipes were stamped with names, so the
city could see who a pipe belonged to.

87
00:04:00,259 --> 00:04:02,499
Frontinus started tearing
out the illegal ones,

88
00:04:02,539 --> 00:04:05,789
and measuring how much water
each aqueduct really delivered.

89
00:04:08,693 --> 00:04:11,143
For more than eight hundred
years, the water kept flowing.

90
00:04:11,273 --> 00:04:12,383
And then, it stopped.

91
00:04:13,010 --> 00:04:16,650
In five thirty-seven AD, a
Gothic army besieged Rome,

92
00:04:16,690 --> 00:04:19,440
and cut the aqueducts to
take away the city's water.

93
00:04:19,868 --> 00:04:21,838
And then the Goths tried something clever.

94
00:04:22,068 --> 00:04:24,508
With the water gone, the
aqueduct channels were empty,

95
00:04:24,548 --> 00:04:27,048
and they tried to send
soldiers through one of them,

96
00:04:27,088 --> 00:04:28,198
right into the city.

97
00:04:28,588 --> 00:04:30,518
The Roman commander had expected it.

98
00:04:30,628 --> 00:04:32,658
He had already sealed
the channel with masonry.

99
00:04:33,655 --> 00:04:37,285
After the war, nobody had the money
or the workers to repair them.

100
00:04:37,515 --> 00:04:40,205
The channels filled with
mineral deposits and rubble,

101
00:04:40,295 --> 00:04:42,625
and one by one, the aqueducts went dry.

102
00:04:43,505 --> 00:04:47,145
Rome shrank from a city of hundreds
of thousands into a small town,

103
00:04:47,185 --> 00:04:48,555
huddled along the river.

104
00:04:48,865 --> 00:04:52,635
For centuries, its people went back to
drinking from the Tiber and from wells.

105
00:04:53,579 --> 00:04:56,069
Some aqueducts ended up
as building material.

106
00:04:56,439 --> 00:05:00,879
In Germany, the aqueduct of Cologne had
built up a thick layer of mineral deposit

107
00:05:00,919 --> 00:05:02,189
inside its channel.

108
00:05:02,459 --> 00:05:05,669
In the Middle Ages, people cut
it out, polished it like marble,

109
00:05:05,819 --> 00:05:06,889
and used it in churches.

110
00:05:07,930 --> 00:05:10,940
It took almost a thousand
years to bring the water back.

111
00:05:11,190 --> 00:05:15,020
In the fourteen hundreds, popes
began to restore the old Aqua Virgo.

112
00:05:15,170 --> 00:05:18,990
And in seventeen sixty-two, the Trevi
Fountain was finished at the end of its line,

113
00:05:19,030 --> 00:05:21,140
as a grand monument to that water.

114
00:05:22,047 --> 00:05:24,197
And the coins people throw into it today?

115
00:05:24,367 --> 00:05:26,137
They're collected and given to charity.

116
00:05:26,347 --> 00:05:28,157
Well over a million euros a year.

117
00:05:30,924 --> 00:05:33,554
Some Roman aqueducts
never needed rebuilding.

118
00:05:33,944 --> 00:05:38,624
In Segovia, in Spain, an aqueduct bridge
of more than twenty thousand granite blocks

119
00:05:38,664 --> 00:05:40,134
stands without any mortar.

120
00:05:40,364 --> 00:05:43,314
It carried water into the city
until the nineteen seventies.

121
00:05:43,593 --> 00:05:48,413
And in twenty twenty-three, scientists at
MIT found that Roman concrete can heal its

122
00:05:48,453 --> 00:05:52,003
own cracks, thanks to small
white lumps of lime inside it.

123
00:05:54,792 --> 00:05:56,522
So, how did ancient Rome get its water?

124
00:05:57,026 --> 00:06:00,676
It found clean springs far away,
and let gravity do the rest,

125
00:06:00,746 --> 00:06:01,756
mostly underground.

126
00:06:02,106 --> 00:06:05,956
It gave the water away for free, and it
fought the thieves who tried to steal it.

127
00:06:06,766 --> 00:06:08,996
And when the water
stopped, Rome stopped too.

128
00:06:09,186 --> 00:06:11,976
It took the city almost a
thousand years to get it back.

129
00:06:12,627 --> 00:06:14,557
And in Rome, water is still free.

130
00:06:14,847 --> 00:06:17,247
More than two and a half
thousand little street fountains,

131
00:06:17,287 --> 00:06:19,337
called nasoni, run day and night.

132
00:06:19,527 --> 00:06:20,717
Anyone can drink from them.

133
00:06:21,036 --> 00:06:24,176
So, would you drink from a
two-thousand-year-old aqueduct?

134
00:06:24,216 --> 00:06:26,186
And which city should we explain next?

135
00:06:26,296 --> 00:06:27,286
Tell me in the comments.

136
00:06:27,676 --> 00:06:28,826
This was How It's Run.
